### How do you sort strings by their length?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class SortByLength {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> codes = List.of(
            "TX1", "UPI123456", "AB9", "NEFT78", "RTGS12345678");

        // Ascending: shortest first
        List<String> ascending = codes.stream()
            .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("Ascending: " + ascending);

        // Descending: longest first
        List<String> descending = codes.stream()
            .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length).reversed())
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("Descending: " + descending);
    }
}
```

```text
Ascending: [TX1, AB9, NEFT78, UPI123456, RTGS12345678]
Descending: [RTGS12345678, UPI123456, NEFT78, TX1, AB9]
```

### How do you make equal-length strings deterministic?

```java
// Equal-length strings sorted alphabetically
List<String> deterministic = codes.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length)
        .thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder()))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

System.out.println(deterministic);
// [AB9, TX1, NEFT78, UPI123456, RTGS12345678]
// AB9 and TX1 are both length 3; alphabetical tie-break puts AB9 first
```

### How is comparingInt different from comparing for length?

| Method | Boxing | Performance |
|---|---|---|
| comparingInt(String::length) | No | Better |
| comparing(String::length) | Yes, int → Integer | Slower on large lists |

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: forgetting a tie-breaker, making equal-length output non-deterministic.

```java
// Non-deterministic: TX1 and AB9 (both length 3) can appear in either order
codes.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// Deterministic: alphabetical tie-breaker
codes.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length)
        .thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder()))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### What about sorting by a different derived int key?

The same technique works for any computed int property.

```java
// Sort account numbers by digit count
List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR12345678", "SAV99");

accounts.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(s -> s.replaceAll("\\D", "").length()))
    .forEach(System.out::println);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- `comparingInt` avoids boxing and is the correct choice for int-returning key extractors.
- Always add a deterministic tie-breaker for reports that must be reproducible.
- The source list is never mutated.
- `String::length` is O(1) because length is a stored field, so the sort overhead is purely the comparison.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you sort longest-first?" Append `.reversed()`.
- "What about equal-length strings?" Add `thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder())`.
- "Can you sort by a different derived key?" Yes, pass any int-returning lambda to `comparingInt`.
